WN.chunk(3,{"n":[[80,"Draw the pre-writing shapes that letters are built from, starting in the right place and going the right way: a line down from the top (|), a line across from the left (-), a circle that starts at the top, goes round to the left (anticlockwise, the way c, a, o and d go) and joins up, and a cross (+) made of a line down and then a line across that meet in the middle; first by watching a grown-up draw it (imitating), then from a finished picture (copying)",["Draws a line down from the top and a line across from the left","Draws a closed circle starting at the top and going round to the left","Copies a cross whose two lines meet in the middle"],[]],[81,"Sit correctly at a table holding a pencil comfortably and correctly; form lower-case letters in the correct direction starting and finishing in the right place",["Hold a pencil with a comfortable tripod or near-tripod grip","Write all 26 lowercase letters with correct starting points and direction","Maintain consistent letter size on lined paper"],["uk-nc-2013:Eng_Y1_WH_01","uk-nc-2013:Eng_Y1_WH_02"]],[82,"Form capital letters correctly; print many upper- and lowercase letters legibly",["Write all 26 capital letters with correct formation","Distinguish between uppercase and lowercase forms in own writing","Use appropriate sizing so capitals are taller than lowercase letters"],["ccss-ela:L.K.1a","uk-nc-2013:Eng_Y1_WH_03"]],[83,"Understand which letters belong to which handwriting families based on similar formation patterns and practise letters in groups",["Group letters by similar strokes (e.g. c, a, d, g, o family — all start with a curve)","Explain why certain letters are practised together","Form letters within the same family consistently using the shared movement pattern"],["uk-nc-2013:Eng_Y1_WH_05"]],[84,"Form digits 0-9 correctly and legibly",["Write all digits 0-9 clearly with correct starting points","Form digits the appropriate size relative to letter height","Write numbers legibly so they can be read by others"],["uk-nc-2013:Eng_Y1_WH_04"]],[85,"Begin to join letters using diagonal and horizontal strokes, understanding which letter pairs are best left unjoined, forming letters of correct size relative to one another",["Join 'in', 'un', 'it' with a diagonal stroke connecting the letters","Leave letters like 'b' unjoined when followed by certain letters, explaining why","Write with consistent letter sizing on lined paper, ascenders and descenders in proportion"],["ccss-ela:L.1.1a","uk-nc-2013:Eng.UKS2.Write.Trans.Hand.1a","uk-nc-2013:Eng.UKS2.Write.Trans.Hand.1b","uk-nc-2013:Eng/KS1/Y2/H/1","uk-nc-2013:Eng/KS1/Y2/H/2","uk-nc-2013:Eng/KS1/Y2/H/3","uk-nc-2013:Eng/KS1/Y2/H/4","uk-nc-2013:Eng_LKS2_Write_Trans_Hand_1","uk-nc-2013:Eng_LKS2_Write_Trans_Hand_2"]]],"r":[[2,"Capital letter formation supports correct sizing in joined writing"],[3,"Joining letters builds on Y1 lowercase letter formation"],[779,"Need to form letters before grouping into families"],[1080,"Master lowercase formation before capitals"],[1707,"Same motor skills for digit formation"],[5135,"Children first draw a shape by watching a grown-up draw it, then copy it"],[5136,"Making marks on purpose comes before controlling the shape of each mark"],[5234,"Holding a pencil for writing follows early mark-making"],[5270,"Lines down from the top and circles that go round to the left are the moves a well-held pencil then makes for letters"]],"c":{"English|Handwriting & Transcription|5":"Your child is mastering the physical skills of writing — learning to form letters and numbers correctly, hold a pencil properly, and develop neat, legible handwriting.","English|Handwriting & Transcription|6":"Your child is learning to join letters together when writing, developing smooth handwriting by connecting letters with diagonal and horizontal strokes while keeping letters the right size.","English|Handwriting & Transcription|3":"Your child is learning the pre-writing shapes that letters are made from: a line down from the top, a line across from the left, a circle that starts at the top and goes round to the left, and a cross made of a line down and a line across that meet in the middle."}});
